    "body": "Internal calls that use `fetchLazy` result in an ExecuteListener's `end` not being called. For example,\n\n``` java\ndb.selectCount().from(USER).fetchOne().value1();\n```\n\nuses a cursor to fetch the first record (see `AbstractResultQuery`). The `Utils.fetchOne(fetchLazy())` does not close the cursor nor indicate to the listener that the lifecycle has completed. This lazy behavior is not documented nor is there a way for the application to indicate to the lifecycle that the `end` should be called.\n\nThe eager version must be used instead,\n\n``` java\ndb.selectCount().from(USER).fetch().get(0).value1();\n```\n\nbut this is in no way obvious.\n\nThis results in connection leaks when using the execute's lifecycle to manage transactions in a Spring transaction aware connection provider.\n\nSee [gist](https://gist.github.com/ben-manes/5316351) for example.\n",
